WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.140
The feds are suing Amazon because they say&nbsp;
it takes too many clicks to cancel a Prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:07.320
Membership. Reason investigated&nbsp;
and here's what we found out.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.960
It took less than 30 seconds and a total&nbsp;
of six clicks to cancel an Amazon Prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.240
membership. That doesn't seem so bad but let's&nbsp;
put it in context. The agency suing Amazon is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.120
the Federal Trade Commission, which is&nbsp;
responsible for consumer protection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.960
So how many clicks does it take to&nbsp;
submit a public comment to the FTC?

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.280
As Twitter user Patrick Hedger points out,&nbsp;
that's more clicks than the FTC complains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.000
about in their lawsuit against Amazon. And&nbsp;
Reason's Eric Boehm noted that there's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.960
number of clicks you can make to get rid of&nbsp;
your FTC subscription. Businesses make our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.220
lives better by efficiently delivering affordable&nbsp;
products through voluntary exchange. Government?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:58.620
They take our money and waste it. So who would you&nbsp;
rather do business with, Amazon or the government?

